---

// Editorial page: fully static, rendered at build time.
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <header class="page-head container--wide">
    <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
    <h1>About This Independent Parking Fury Resource</h1>
    <p>
      What this site is, where its facts come from, why the official games are not re-hosted here,
      and how the original games were made. Short version: one player, original writing,
      official sources, nothing hidden.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section container prose">
    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>
      A guide to the Parking Fury series — controls, version differences, level structure,
      troubleshooting and a curated list of alternatives — plus a small
      <a href="/games/">games library</a> of original games that play right on this site. It
      exists because searching for any of those things mostly returns the game portals themselves
      or thin “unblocked” mirror sites — there is little in between that simply explains how the
      games work.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is <strong>not</strong> affiliated with the game’s creator, Andriy Pidvirnyy, or with
      Coolmath Games, Poki, Brain Software or any other platform. No official endorsement exists
      or is implied; “Parking Fury” belongs to its rights holder and is used here only to refer
      to the games being documented.
    </p>

    <h2>Where every fact comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Factual claims about the series are checked against the pages of the platforms that license
      them — Coolmath Games for the top-down trilogy, Poki for the 3D series — and each guide page
      states when it was checked (currently <strong>October 2026</strong>). Specifically:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Release dates, controls, level counts, platforms:</strong> taken verbatim or closely paraphrased from the official game pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Level counts for the sequels:</strong> not published, therefore not claimed.</li>
      <li><strong>Related-game descriptions:</strong> rewritten from each host’s own description — not presented as our reviews, because we did not review them here.</li>
      <li><strong>Anything we cannot source</strong> is left out rather than filled in with plausible-sounding detail.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Spot something wrong? <a href="/contact/">Corrections are welcome</a> — accuracy beats
      volume on a site like this.
    </p>

    <h2 id="embeds">Why the official games are not embedded here</h2>
    <p>
      Embedding a game means your page loads the host’s copy inside a frame. That is only
      legitimate with the rights holder’s permission — and as of October 2026, both official
      hosts of the series actively block it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Coolmath Games</strong> responds with <code>X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN</code>, so its game pages refuse to render inside other sites.</li>
      <li><strong>Poki</strong> sends <code>Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors https://*.poki.io …</code>, which allows framing only from its own infrastructure.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So this site sends you to the licensed copies instead of pirating a mirror, and the version
      pages’ game panels stay empty for the same reason. The panel has a real integration point:
      if a licensed embed is ever granted, the operator adds it in the admin dashboard and the
      same panel loads the official game with loading, timeout and error states already built in.
    </p>

    <h2>The original games are ours</h2>
    <p>
      “Tight Lot” (three-lot parking) and “Traffic Rush” (endless lane dodging) were written for
      this site from scratch: original code, original canvas rendering, no third-party libraries
      or art. They make no network requests, play no sound, and store only your best results in
      your own browser’s local storage. Each plays inside its own page here — in a frame on its
      game page — and never redirects you to another website.
    </p>

    <h2>How the site is funded and tracked</h2>
    <p>
      It is not funded by anything — there are no ads, no trackers, no third-party scripts and no
      visitor accounts. The only cookie on the whole site is the operator’s own dashboard session
      (described in the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>), which visitors never receive. The
      policy also spells out the two same-origin counters the dashboard uses to compare traffic
      with Search Console — first-party, cookie-free, and nothing like analytics tooling. There is
      no consent banner to click through because there is nothing else for you to consent to.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial rules we hold ourselves to</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No fabricated ratings, reviews, testimonials, statistics or author credentials.</li>
      <li>No fake download buttons, fake “play” controls or misleading redirects — every button does what its label says.</li>
      <li>Only games the operator has the right to embed are published; official hosts that block framing (Coolmath Games, Poki) are linked to, never framed.</li>
      <li>No walkthrough steps for levels we have not verified, and no claims of “every level covered” until they are true.</li>
      <li>No doorway pages: a URL exists only when it answers a genuinely different question.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/games/">Play</a>
      <a class="btn btn--secondary" href="/contact/">Contact &amp; corrections</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
